{
  "id": "COMPOUND:980 - PROTEIN:5136",
  "verbalization": "Doxorubicin is implicated in the interaction with NCI-H209 through a multi-step pathway involving receptor-targeting mechanisms. The evidence suggests that doxorubicin acts as a target for multiple receptors, with one direct pathway linking it to NCI-H209 via the Probable G-protein coupled receptor 132, which is targeted by Racotumomab. Another pathway connects doxorubicin to NCI-H209 through the P2X purinoceptor 1, which is targeted by a compound that has been shown to interact with NCI-H209. The third pathway involves doxorubicin targeting NCI-H46, which in turn targets the same compound that interacts with NCI-H209. These findings collectively suggest that doxorubicin may exert its effects on NCI-H209 through receptor-mediated interactions, possibly involving different signaling pathways. The integration of these pathways highlights the complexity of the relationship between doxorubicin and NCI-H209, emphasizing the role of receptor-targeting in drug action.",
